{
  "title": "Flaky tests: reliability bug, not test noise",
  "content": "The flaky tests advice that circulates internally rarely matches what's in the public docs. This is the writeup that includes the part that usually goes wrong. The edge cases only show up once real users hit the system.\n\n## The one-paragraph version (read this first)\n\nA flaky test is a test that passes and fails on the same code. The instinct is to treat it as a testing problem — rerun it, tag it, skip it, move on. That instinct is wrong. A flaky test is a signal that something in the system is non-deterministic, and non-determinism does not care whether it is being observed by a test. The same race condition that makes an assertion fail intermittently will, under different timing, corrupt a record, drop a message, or return stale data to a user. The test is not the problem; the test is the smoke detector. This post explains why flakiness is a production reliability signal, how to classify the underlying causes, and what to do in the next 30 minutes to start treating it that way.\n\n## Why this concept confuses people\n\nThe confusion starts with where the failure appears. When a test fails, the failure surfaces in the test suite. When it passes on rerun, the suite goes green. The feedback loop teaches a specific lesson: the test is unreliable. From there, the natural fixes are all test-side — increase the timeout, add a retry, mark it `@flaky`, quarantine it. Each of those fixes makes the suite greener without touching the code under test.\n\nThere is a second source of confusion: the word \"flaky\" is used for at least four different phenomena that have almost nothing in common. A test that depends on wall-clock time, a test that depends on shared mutable state, a test that depends on network ordering, and a test that depends on an unseeded random number generator all get filed under the same label. They need different fixes, and only some of them indicate a production bug.\n\nThe third source is organizational. Test suites are usually owned by whoever wrote them, and flaky tests are usually owned by nobody. A failing test blocks a merge, so the fastest path to unblocking is to make the test stop failing. The incentive structure rewards silencing the signal, not investigating it. That is a process problem masquerading as a technical one.\n\nThe real problem this post solves: teams routinely delete or quarantine the one signal that would have told them, weeks before an incident, that a code path was timing-dependent.\n\n## The mental model that makes it click\n\nThink of a test suite like a canary in a coal mine. The canary is not the hazard; the canary is the sensor. If the canary dies, you do not replace the canary and go back to work — you evacuate. A flaky test is a canary that dies intermittently. Intermittent death is not better news than consistent death; it is arguably worse, because it tells you the hazard is timing-dependent and therefore harder to reproduce.\n\nThe useful reframe: a test is a deterministic function of the system state. If the same code and the same inputs produce different outputs, then either the inputs are not the same (hidden state) or the function is not deterministic (a race, an unseeded RNG, a clock dependency, an external service). Both of those are properties of the system, not of the test.\n\nThere is one important exception. A test can be flaky because the *test itself* is badly written — for example, it asserts on a log line that is written asynchronously by a background thread the test does not join. In that case the flakiness is genuinely test-side. The skill is telling the two apart, and the default assumption should be that the system is at fault until proven otherwise. Test-side flakiness is real but rarer than teams assume, and assuming it first is how real bugs get buried.\n\nA useful heuristic: if the test fails in CI but passes locally, ask what is different about the environments. If the answer is \"nothing I can name,\" that is itself the finding — you have an unnamed environmental dependency.\n\n## The four families of flakiness\n\nAlmost every flaky test falls into one of four families. The family determines both the production risk and the fix.\n\n### 1. Time and clock dependencies\n\nThe test uses the real clock — `System.currentTimeMillis()`, `time.time()`, `Date.now()`, `datetime.now()` — or depends on a timeout that is too tight for a loaded CI machine. The production risk depends on whether the production code also uses the real clock in a way that matters. A test that sleeps 100ms and asserts a result is test-side flakiness. A test that exposes a production code path using `now()` to decide whether a token is expired is a production bug: the same code will misjudge expiry under load.\n\n### 2. Shared mutable state and ordering\n\nTests that share a database, a cache, a global singleton, or a temp directory can interfere with each other. When tests run in parallel or in a different order, interference changes. The production risk is high: shared mutable state in tests usually mirrors shared mutable state in production, and the same interleaving that breaks a test can break a request.\n\n### 3. Concurrency and races\n\nTests that start a thread, a goroutine, an async task, or a background worker and then assert on its output without a proper join or synchronization primitive. These are the highest-signal flakes. A race the test can hit is a race production can hit; production just has more traffic and more chances.\n\n### 4. External dependencies and network\n\nTests that hit a real network, a real queue, a real third-party API, or a real DNS resolver. Some of this is unavoidable in integration tests, but if a test is flaky because a dependency is slow, the fix is usually to inject a fake — which also makes production code more testable and more resilient. The production risk is moderate: the system may genuinely be fragile to dependency latency, in which case the flake is telling you something.\n\n| Family | Typical symptom | Production risk | Usual fix |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Clock | Passes locally, fails in CI around midnight or on slow machines | Low if test-only, high if production uses real clock for decisions | Inject a clock; make time a parameter |\n| Shared state | Fails only when run with other tests, passes in isolation | High — mirrors production shared state | Isolate state per test; use transactions or unique namespaces |\n| Concurrency | Fails rarely, often on CI with different core counts | Highest — same race exists in production | Fix the race in production code; do not paper over with sleeps |\n| External deps | Fails when a dependency is slow or down | Moderate — may indicate missing timeouts or retries | Inject fakes; add timeouts and circuit breakers in production |\n\n## A concrete worked example\n\nConsider a small Python service that processes jobs from a queue and writes results to a database. A test asserts that after enqueueing a job, the result row exists within one second.\n\n```python\nimport time\nimport threading\nfrom queue import Queue\n\nclass Worker:\n    def __init__(self, db):\n        self.db = db\n        self.queue = Queue()\n        self._stop = threading.Event()\n        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self._run, daemon=True)\n\n    def start(self):\n        self._thread.start()\n\n    def stop(self):\n        self._stop.set()\n        self._thread.join(timeout=2)\n\n    def enqueue(self, job):\n        self.queue.put(job)\n\n    def _run(self):\n        while not self._stop.is_set():\n            try:\n                job = self.queue.get(timeout=0.1)\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            result = process(job)\n            self.db.write(result)\n```\n\nA naive test:\n\n```python\ndef test_job_writes_result(db):\n    worker = Worker(db)\n    worker.start()\n    worker.enqueue({\"id\": 1})\n    time.sleep(1)  # hope the worker finishes\n    assert db.get(1) is not None\n```\n\nThis test is flaky on a loaded CI machine. The instinct is to raise the sleep to five seconds. That makes the flake rarer without fixing anything. The real issue is that the test has no way to know when the write has happened, and the production code has no way to signal it. Add a completion event:\n\n```python\nclass Worker:\n    # ... same as before ...\n    def __init__(self, db):\n        self.db = db\n        self.queue = Queue()\n        self._stop = threading.Event()\n        self._done = {}  # job id -> threading.Event\n        self._lock = threading.Lock()\n        self._thread = threading.Thread(target=self._run, daemon=True)\n\n    def enqueue(self, job):\n        with self._lock:\n            self._done[job[\"id\"]] = threading.Event()\n        self.queue.put(job)\n\n    def wait_for(self, job_id, timeout=5):\n        with self._lock:\n            ev = self._done.get(job_id)\n        if ev is None:\n            raise KeyError(job_id)\n        if not ev.wait(timeout):\n            raise TimeoutError(f\"job {job_id} did not complete\")\n\n    def _run(self):\n        while not self._stop.is_set():\n            try:\n                job = self.queue.get(timeout=0.1)\n            except Exception:\n                continue\n            result = process(job)\n            self.db.write(result)\n            with self._lock:\n                ev = self._done.get(job[\"id\"])\n            if ev is not None:\n                ev.set()\n```\n\nAnd the test:\n\n```python\ndef test_job_writes_result(db):\n    worker = Worker(db)\n    worker.start()\n    worker.enqueue({\"id\": 1})\n    worker.wait_for(1, timeout=5)\n    assert db.get(1) is not None\n```\n\nNotice what changed. The test is now deterministic. More importantly, the production code now has a way to report completion, which means callers can wait for a job instead of polling or sleeping. The flaky test was pointing at a missing capability in the production API. Fixing the test properly fixed the production code.\n\nThis is the pattern to internalize: a flaky test is often an interface that has not been designed yet. The test is trying to synchronize with something the production code does not expose.\n\n## How this connects to things you already know\n\nIf you have ever debugged a production incident that \"only happens under load,\" you have already met the family of bugs that flaky tests detect. Load is a timing amplifier. A race that needs a specific interleaving to manifest appears rarely in tests and frequently in production, because production runs the code path millions of times.\n\nThe connection to observability is also direct. A flaky test is essentially a unit-level alert. If you would not silence a production alert by deleting the alert rule, you should not silence a flaky test by deleting the test. Both are the same move: removing the sensor because you do not like the reading.\n\nThe connection to CI cost is worth naming too. Teams often justify quarantining flaky tests by pointing at wasted CI minutes. That is a real cost, but it is the smaller one. The larger cost is the incident that ships because the test that would have caught it was quarantined six weeks earlier. CI minutes are visible; averted incidents are not, which is exactly why the trade-off is misjudged.\n\n## Common misconceptions, corrected\n\n**\"It's just the test environment.\"** Sometimes. But the test environment is also a computer running your code. If the code behaves differently on a loaded machine, it will behave differently on a loaded production machine. The environment is part of the system.\n\n**\"Retries make flaky tests fine.\"** Retries make the *suite* green. They do not make the *code* deterministic. A retry is a bet that the flake is environmental, and that bet is wrong often enough that it should be a deliberate decision, not a default. If you do add retries, log every retry with the test name and the failure reason, and treat a rising retry rate as a production risk indicator.\n\n**\"Flaky tests are a test-suite problem.\"** They are a test-suite *symptom*. The problem lives in the code under test until you prove otherwise.\n\n**\"We'll fix it later.\"** Later is when the test is deleted by someone under deadline pressure who has no context. If a flake is not triaged within a sprint, the default outcome is deletion, not repair.\n\n**\"It only fails 1% of the time, so it's low priority.\"** A 1% flake rate on a code path that runs a million times a day is ten thousand failures a day. The flake rate of the test and the failure rate of the production path are not the same number, and the production number is usually much larger.\n\n## The advanced version (once the basics are solid)\n\nOnce you accept that flaky tests are reliability signals, the question becomes how to triage them systematically. A workable framework has three axes: blast radius, reproducibility, and family.\n\n**Blast radius** asks what production code the test exercises. A flake in a test of a pure function is low blast radius. A flake in a test of a payment state machine is high blast radius. Prioritize by blast radius, not by flake frequency.\n\n**Reproducibility** asks whether you can make the flake deterministic. The best tool here is not more test runs — it is a deterministic scheduler or a fault-injection harness. Some languages and runtimes offer deterministic concurrency testing tools; if yours does not, you can still force orderings by injecting synchronization points into the code under test behind a test-only flag. The goal is to turn a 1-in-1000 flake into a 1-in-1 reproduction.\n\n**Family** tells you the fix. Clock flakes get a clock injection. Shared-state flakes get isolation. Concurrency flakes get the race fixed. External-dependency flakes get fakes and production timeouts.\n\nA practical triage policy that teams have found workable:\n\n- Every flaky test gets an owner and an issue within one working day. No anonymous flakes.\n- Quarantine is allowed only with a linked issue, an owner, and an expiry date. Quarantine without an expiry is deletion with extra steps.\n- Retries are allowed only on tests explicitly classified as external-dependency flakes, and the retry count and reason are logged.\n- A flake in a high-blast-radius test blocks the release until triaged, the same way a failing test does.\n\nThere is also a measurement question. You cannot manage what you do not count. Most CI systems expose test-level pass/fail history; the useful metric is not total flake count but flake rate per test, tracked over time. A test whose flake rate is rising is a leading indicator of a production problem in the area it covers.\n\n## Frequently asked questions\n\n**How do I tell if a flaky test is a production bug or just a bad test?**\nAsk whether the test's non-determinism comes from the code under test or from the test harness. If the production code uses a real clock, shared state, or unsynchronized concurrency, the flake is a production signal. If the test only fails because it asserts on something asynchronous it does not join, it is test-side. The default assumption should be production until proven otherwise.\n\n**Why do flaky tests often pass locally but fail in CI?**\nCI machines are usually more loaded, have different core counts, and run tests in a different order or in parallel. All of those change timing and interleaving, which is exactly what non-deterministic code is sensitive to. The difference between local and CI is not a reason to dismiss the flake; it is a clue about which family it belongs to.\n\n**Is it ever acceptable to delete a flaky test?**\nOnly after triage has shown the test is genuinely test-side and the cost of fixing it exceeds the value of keeping it. Deleting before triage is the failure mode this whole post is about. If you must remove a test, link the issue that explains why, so the decision is reviewable later.\n\n**What is the single best first step for a team with many flaky tests?**\nStop allowing silent retries. Make every retry log the test name and failure reason, and make the retry rate visible on a dashboard. That one change converts an invisible problem into a measurable one, and measurement is the precondition for every other fix.\n\n## Quick reference\n\n- A flaky test is a non-determinism detector, not a test bug.\n- Four families: clock, shared state, concurrency, external dependency.\n- Highest production risk: concurrency and shared state.\n- Default assumption: the system is at fault, not the test.\n- Retries hide the signal; if you use them, log every one.\n- Quarantine requires an owner, an issue, and an expiry date.\n- Triage by blast radius first, frequency second.\n- The fix for a flaky test is often a missing production interface.\n\n## Further reading worth your time\n\n- The Google Testing Blog has published several posts on flaky test management and quarantine policies; search for \"flaky tests\" on their site.\n- The `pytest` documentation covers `pytest-rerunfailures` and fixture scoping, which are the two most common sources of test-side flakiness in Python suites.\n- For concurrency specifically, look for deterministic scheduler tooling in your language ecosystem — for example, `loom` in Rust or `jcstress` on the JVM — and read their examples; they are written to expose exactly the races that flaky tests stumble into.\n\n## Do this in the next 30 minutes\n\nOpen your CI configuration and find where test retries are configured. If retries are on and silent, turn on logging for them, or turn them off entirely for one pipeline run. Then look at the last week of CI history and list every test that failed and passed on rerun. That list is your reliability backlog — not your test backlog. Pick the top item by blast radius and open an issue with an owner today.\n\n\n---\n\n### About this article\n\n**Written by:** [Kubai Kevin](/about/) — software developer based in Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n**How this article was produced:** This site uses an automated LLM pipeline designed and maintained by the author. Drafts pass automated quality gates (minimum length, uniqueness, concrete metrics, versioned tools, code samples, absence of filler). Individual line-by-line human editing is not performed on every post before publication. Figures, benchmarks and scenarios are illustrative unless a source is linked; verify them against official documentation before relying on them in production. See the AI content policy.\n\n**Corrections:** Report errors via the [contact page](/contact/). Corrections are applied promptly.\n\n**Last generated:** October 2026\n",
